---

ogygia installs into an existing SvelteKit app. You add three parts once (the package, the Vite plugin and the server handle) and then adopt ogygia one route at a time, so every route you have not converted stays a normal Kit page.

## Setup

The CLI does the whole setup in one command: it installs the package, registers the Vite plugin before `sveltekit()`, and adds the server handle and the transport.

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npx ogygia init
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm dlx ogygia init
```

:::

To add markdown content collections at the same time, run `npx ogygia init --markdown`. If your project already exports a `handle` or a `transport`, the CLI merges into them instead of overwriting them: it puts your `handle` in a sequence and spreads into your `transport`.

The command adds `ogygia` to your dependencies, plus `@ogygia/content` with `--markdown`, and adds `@ogygia/cli` to your devDependencies so that later `npx ogygia` commands run your local copy. It installs none of the other feature packages; you add those yourself, as described in [Other features](#transport-held-regions-over-the-wire-other-features-optional). The [CLI reference](/docs/reference/cli) lists every command.

### Manual setup

If you prefer to add the parts yourself, install the package, register the Vite plugin **before** `sveltekit()`, and add the server handle.

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i ogygia
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add ogygia
```

:::

#### vite.config.ts

`ogygia()` must run before `sveltekit()`, and the plugin also sets `enforce: 'pre'`. In a monorepo, the plugin adds its package root to Vite's `server.fs.allow`, so Vite does not block the shims and runtime it resolves from outside the app directory. The [API reference](/docs/reference/api) covers each option.

```ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [ogygia(), sveltekit()] // order matters
});
```

#### hooks.server.ts

`ogygia.handle()` serves the signed island endpoint that `render: 'deferred'` and `render: 'live'` regions fetch from. Its default path is `/__ogygia__`. If you have other handles, combine them with `sequence()`.

To change the path, pass `ogygia.handle({ endpoint: '/my-islands' })`. The handle then answers there, and every hole on your pages asks there. Pick a path your deployment actually sends to the app: behind a proxy that only forwards some prefixes, put it under one of them (for example `/_server/api/__ogygia__`). Don't put it under Kit's `appDir` (`/_app/…`), because Kit answers those paths itself before any handle runs.

```ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';

export const handle = ogygia.handle();
```

::: warning In production
Server islands and command or form POSTs go through Kit's CSRF protection, so set `ORIGIN` on your deployment. Without it, Kit rejects those POSTs.
:::

#### TypeScript types

ogygia resolves some build-time virtual modules (`virtual:ogygia/*`), and your editor and `svelte-check` need one reference line to see their types. Add a `src/ogygia.d.ts` that contains only this:

```ts
/// <reference types="ogygia/types" />
```

Without it, `svelte-check` reports the virtual imports as unresolved, although the build works either way. `npx ogygia init` writes this file for you.

## Transport (held regions over the wire)

Held regions that render inline need no setup. Some held regions **cross the wire**, though, such as one returned from a search remote or a live content source, and those need the `transport` hook. Install it once in your universal hooks:

```ts title=src/hooks.ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';
export const transport = { ...ogygia.transport };
```

### Markdown, Shiki & mdsvex (optional)

For `.svx` and `.md` [content collections](/docs/content/collections), add the content package, which brings mdsvex and Shiki, and list its `content()` plugin beside `ogygia()`:

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/content
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { content } from '@ogygia/content/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    ogygia(),
    content({ markdown: {} }), // {} = defaults: Shiki + heading ids + collected headings
    sveltekit()
  ]
});
```

Put `extensions` and `preprocess` in **`svelte.config.js`**, not inline on `sveltekit()`. Kit v2, `svelte-check` and the Svelte language server all read `svelte.config.js`, and if you pass the options inline, Kit ignores `svelte.config.js` and `svelte-check` never sees them:

```js title=svelte.config.js
import { vitePreprocess } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';
import { preprocess, extensions } from '@ogygia/content/vite';

export default {
  extensions: extensions(), // includes .svelte, adds .svx / .md
  preprocess: [vitePreprocess(), ...preprocess()]
};
```

`preprocess()` is synchronous and does not load mdsvex while markdown is off; mdsvex loads on first use. `extensions()` adds `.svx` and `.md` only when markdown is on. Themes and headings are set under `content({ markdown })`, which [content collections](/docs/content/collections) describes.

The content macros (`import.meta.og.loader`, `og.code`, `og.md`) bring their own types, so add a second line to `src/ogygia.d.ts`:

```ts
/// <reference types="ogygia/types" />
/// <reference types="@ogygia/content/types" />
```

### Other features (optional)

The other features are set up the same way as markdown. Each is a package you install, with a plugin you list once beside `ogygia()` and before `sveltekit()`, for example `plugins: [ogygia(), flags(), sveltekit()]`:

- **`@ogygia/flags`** (`flags()`): kill switches, rollouts and A/B tests, decided on the server. See [Flags & experiments](/docs/app/experiments).
- **`@ogygia/postrender`** (`postrender()`): pages stored whole on their first request and served as bytes. See [Postrender](/docs/data-state/postrender).
- **`@ogygia/federation`** (`federation()`): other teams' apps rendered into your pages. See [Fragment federation](/docs/app/federation).
- **`@ogygia/router`** (`router()`): routes declared as values in code. See [Server router](/docs/app/server-router).
- **`@ogygia/devtools`** (`devtools()`): the in-page devtools dock and the SSR profiler. See [Devtools](/docs/devtools/overview) and [Profiler](/docs/profiler/overview).
- **`@ogygia/testing`** (no plugin): Playwright helpers. See [Testing](/docs/devtools/testing).

Some of these also need a line in `hooks.server.ts`, such as postrender's handle or the router's. [Plugins](/docs/reference/plugins) gives each package's install command, Vite line and server wiring.

## Adopt one route at a time

You don't need to convert the whole app. ogygia is incremental, so you can put islands on one route and leave every other route a normal SvelteKit page. A route that is almost static gains the most from `csr = false` plus islands. A route that is a full interactive app can stay `csr = true` and still use an island where it helps. A site made entirely of static-first pages can go all-islands. You choose per route, not once for the whole app.

### One route

To convert a route, remove the Kit client from it and mark its islands. The other routes do not change. The [Overview](/docs/start/overview) explains what `csr = false` does and why islands work without the Kit client.

```ts title=src/routes/marketing/+page.ts
export const csr = false;
```

### The router is global

The SPA router is on by default for the whole app, and there is no component to render. On routes with `csr = true`, the Kit client owns navigation and ogygia does not intercept clicks there, so the two coexist. To turn the router off, use `ogygia({ router: false })`. See [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router).

### Islands on a normal Kit page

An island does not need `csr = false`. Marked imports work on a `csr = true` Kit page as well: the component becomes an island and the rest of the page hydrates the Kit way. What such a page ships depends on what it rendered, not on what it imports. A marked component's client wrapper loads its component only when the server rendered that island (a stamp in the head records this), so a `csr = true` route that imports a registry of many marked blocks links the stylesheet and chunk of the blocks on the page, not of every block in the registry. A block that first appears after a client-side navigation loads on demand.

### All-islands apps

You can also remove the Kit client from the whole app. When **every** route has `csr = false`, Kit usually skips its client build, so ogygia detects this and runs a standalone islands build to keep your island chunks shipping.

You do **not** need a keepalive route for this. When Kit would skip its client build, the Vite plugin injects a hidden route with no URL that exists only during the build and is removed afterwards, so islands work without any placeholder files in your project.

## Do / don't

| Do                                                                                                 | Don't                                                                                           |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Start with your most static, highest-traffic route, where removing the Kit client gains the most.  | Don't set the whole app to `csr = false` on day one. Convert route by route and measure.        |
| Register `ogygia()` **before** `sveltekit()`. Order matters, and the plugin sets `enforce: 'pre'`. | Don't add a placeholder `csr = true` page to keep an all-islands build alive. It is not needed. |
| Keep truly app-like routes on `csr = true`. ogygia works alongside them.                           | Don't forget `ORIGIN` in production. Server-island and form POSTs need it.                      |
